winhttp: run a request synchronously when no status callback is installed (Adobe Help, Neutron#13)

Every Help menu page in Lightroom Classic ("Library Module Help..." and the rest) failed with
"Cannot display help." Adobe's Help client (ahclient.dll, shipped with Lightroom Classic, Premiere
Pro, Audition, InDesign, Animate and Media Encoder) checks it is online by opening a WinHTTP session
with WINHTTP_FLAG_ASYNC, installing no status callback, and calling WinHttpSendRequest,
WinHttpReceiveResponse and WinHttpQueryHeaders(STATUS_CODE) back to back. Wine queued the operations,
so the status query failed with ERROR_WINHTTP_INCORRECT_HANDLE_STATE (or ReceiveResponse did, when the
queued send had not started yet); the client concluded it was offline, took its local-help path
(ARH.exe, which Adobe does not ship) and gave up without opening a browser.

A request in an async session that has no status callback has nothing to report completion through,
so send, receive, query-data-available, read and write now run synchronously for it. Requests with
a callback keep the asynchronous path unchanged. That Windows completes these calls is inferred
from Adobe's Help working there; Microsoft's documentation only says WinHttpReceiveResponse "can
operate either synchronously or asynchronously" in async mode.

Harness: preserved-fixes/harnesses/winhttp-async-nocallback.c (local HTTP server) -- 11.10-108 fails
the no-callback status query (12019), the patched build passes; the with-callback control passes on
both. Lightroom Classic: Library Module Help now launches winebrowser with Adobe's help page.

--- a/dlls/winhttp/request.c
+++ b/dlls/winhttp/request.c
@@ -2429,6 +2429,21 @@
     return ERROR_SUCCESS;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: whether a request's operations run asynchronously. WINHTTP_FLAG_ASYNC alone is not
+ * enough: with no status callback installed there is nothing to report completion through, and an
+ * application treating such a session as synchronous gets its results only if the calls block.
+ * Adobe's Help client (ahclient.dll, shipped with Lightroom Classic, Premiere, Audition, InDesign ...)
+ * opens its online check with WINHTTP_FLAG_ASYNC and no callback, then calls WinHttpSendRequest,
+ * WinHttpReceiveResponse and WinHttpQueryHeaders(STATUS_CODE) back to back. Queued, the status
+ * query failed with ERROR_WINHTTP_INCORRECT_HANDLE_STATE, the client concluded it was offline, and
+ * every Help menu page failed with "Cannot display help." (Neutron#13). It works on Windows, so
+ * Windows completes these calls; that is inferred from Adobe's code, not documented. Requests
+ * with a callback keep the asynchronous path unchanged. */
+static BOOL request_is_async( struct request *request )
+{
+    return (request->connect->hdr.flags & WINHTTP_FLAG_ASYNC) && request->hdr.callback;
+}
+
 static DWORD send_request( struct request *request, const WCHAR *headers, DWORD headers_len, void *optional,
                            DWORD optional_len, DWORD total_len, DWORD_PTR context, BOOL async )
 {
@@ -2598,7 +2613,7 @@
 
     if (headers && !headers_len) headers_len = lstrlenW( headers );
 
-    if (request->connect->hdr.flags & WINHTTP_FLAG_ASYNC)
+    if (request_is_async( request ))
     {
         struct send_request *s;
 
@@ -3315,7 +3330,7 @@
 
 static DWORD receive_response( struct request *request )
 {
-    BOOL async = request->connect->hdr.flags & WINHTTP_FLAG_ASYNC;
+    BOOL async = request_is_async( request );
     DWORD ret, size, query, status;
 
     TRACE( "request state %d\n", request->state );
@@ -3529,7 +3544,7 @@
         return FALSE;
     }
 
-    if (!(async = request->connect->hdr.flags & WINHTTP_FLAG_ASYNC) || skip_async_queue( request, &wont_block, 1 ))
+    if (!(async = request_is_async( request )) || skip_async_queue( request, &wont_block, 1 ))
     {
         ret = query_data_available( request, available, async );
     }
@@ -3650,7 +3665,7 @@
         return FALSE;
     }
 
-    if (!(async = request->connect->hdr.flags & WINHTTP_FLAG_ASYNC) || skip_async_queue( request, &wont_block, to_read ))
+    if (!(async = request_is_async( request )) || skip_async_queue( request, &wont_block, to_read ))
     {
         ret = read_data( request, buffer, to_read, read, async );
     }
@@ -3763,7 +3778,7 @@
         return FALSE;
     }
 
-    if (!(request->connect->hdr.flags & WINHTTP_FLAG_ASYNC))
+    if (!request_is_async( request ))
     {
         ret = write_data( request, buffer, to_write, written, FALSE );
     }
